From 708df0d2ff963ea9ac32f2b51c854f9f4b55c227 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 15:18:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Let an app that embeds Mudlet tell games its own name

A game is told what the client is called in TTYPE, NEW-ENVIRON's CLIENT_NAME, MSDP's CLIENT_NAME, GMCP's Core.Hello,
ATCP's hello and MXP's <VERSION> reply. Desktop Mudlet says MUDLET in the first two and Mudlet in the rest. An app
built on mudlet_core that is not desktop Mudlet, such as Mudlet for Android, had no way to say so, and games that
read the name to tell clients apart (for their statistics, or for what a client is known to support) took it for
desktop Mudlet.

- THeadlessSession::setClientName(), for the embedder, and MudletApp::setClientName() / clientName() behind it,
  hold one name for every session, "Mudlet" unless set. TTYPE and NEW-ENVIRON send it in capitals and the others as
  it is, as desktop always has, so desktop's bytes are unchanged and an app's "Mudlet-Android" goes out as
  MUDLET-ANDROID in the first two and Mudlet-Android in the rest.
- A runtime setter rather than a compile definition: one binary can be tested both ways, the build scripts that
  compile mudlet_core for an app need nothing new, and there are no #ifdefs in ctelnet.cpp.
- In capitals the name has to be a terminal name RFC 1010 allows, as TTYPE carries it: ASCII letters, digits, '-'
  and '/', at most 40, starting with a letter and ending with a letter or digit. That also keeps out anything
  GMCP's JSON, an MXP attribute or telnet would need escaped. Anything else is refused with false and a warning.
- The version beside it stays Mudlet's, and the HTTP User-Agent is unchanged. No Lua function reads the name, so
  scripts see no difference.
- docs/headless.md says when to call it.

Tested by MudletAppClientNameTest (the default, a set name kept as given, forgetting it, which names are taken and
refused), TMxpVersionTagTest (the <VERSION> reply names the app) and TelnetClientNameTest (TTYPE, NEW-ENVIRON, MSDP,
GMCP and both of ATCP's answers give desktop's names by default, the app's in each protocol's case once set, and
desktop's again once it is forgotten).

